.TH "UBERTOOTH\-SPECAN\-UI" "1" "October 2026" "Project Ubertooth" "User Commands"
.SH "NAME"
\fBubertooth\-specan\-ui\fR \- graphical 2\.4 GHz spectrum analyzer for Ubertooth
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.nf
ubertooth\-specan\-ui [\-U <index|serial>] [\-l <freq>] [\-u <freq>]
[\-\-wifi[=<channel(s)>]] [\-\-padding <MHz>]
ubertooth\-specan\-ui [\-U <index|serial>] \-V
ubertooth\-specan\-ui \-h
.fi
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
ubertooth\-specan\-ui(1) shows the 2\.4 GHz band as a live spectrum plot in a window\. It runs ubertooth\-specan(1) in the background and draws what it reports, so it needs an attached Ubertooth running the \fBbluetooth_rxtx\fR firmware, Python 3\.8 or newer, and the Python packages \fBnumpy\fR, \fBqtpy\fR and a Qt binding (\fBPySide6\fR or \fBPyQt5\fR)\.
.P
To show the whole band on the only attached Ubertooth, run:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-specan\-ui
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
To pick one of several Ubertooths, run:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-specan\-ui \-U 0
.fi
.IP "" 0
.SH "OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-U <index|serial>\fR
select an Ubertooth by enumeration index or serial number; see ubertooth(7), SELECTING A DEVICE
.TP
\fB\-l <2402\-2480>\fR
lower frequency in MHz (default 2402)
.TP
\fB\-u <2402\-2480>\fR
upper frequency in MHz (default 2480); must not be lower than \fB\-l\fR\. The plot is drawn 2 MHz wider than the scanned range on each side, within 2400\-2483 MHz, so the edge channels can be marked
.TP
\fB\-\-wifi[=<channel(s)>]\fR
show the band around one Wi\-Fi channel (for example \fB\-\-wifi=6\fR) or a range of channels (\fB\-\-wifi=1\-11\fR); \fB\-\-wifi\fR alone shows channel 1\. Channel 14 is centered on 2484 MHz, above the 2480 MHz the board can tune, so \fB\-\-wifi=14\fR shows the part of that channel below 2480 MHz \-\- and with a \fB\-\-padding\fR under 4 it is refused, because then the whole band asked for is above the range
.TP
\fB\-\-padding <MHz>\fR
extra band shown on each side with \fB\-\-wifi\fR (default 10), limited to 2402\-2480 MHz
.TP
\fB\-V\fR
print the version and end the command, before the window opens: the host tools, then the firmware version and build of the board \fB\-U\fR names, or of each attached board\. The same answer every other tool gives, including with no board attached; see ubertooth(7), COMMON OPTIONS\. This tool answers it by running ubertooth\-util(1) \fB\-V\fR, so that tool has to be on the \fBPATH\fR, and is reported as \fBerror: could not run ubertooth\-util: No such file or directory\fR when it is not
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
print the usage text on standard output and exit 0\.
.P
\fB\-V\fR and \fB\-h\fR both end the command, and both answer on standard output, so a command line with both prints both: the version first and the usage text last, whichever order they were written in\. The status is then \fB\-V\fR's, so a board that could not be read makes a command line carrying \fB\-h\fR exit 1, with the usage text printed all the same\. Both come after the range checks below, so a range this tool will not take is reported instead\.
.P
The whole command line is read before the window's own modules are imported, so \fB\-V\fR, \fB\-h\fR and a refused range are answered on a machine with no \fBnumpy\fR and no Qt binding\. Only opening the window needs those\.
.SH "DISPLAY"
.IP "\(bu" 4
White trace: the current sweep\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
Green trace: the highest level seen recently (max hold)\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
Red dotted lines: the strongest peak, labeled with its frequency and level\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
Cyan dotted line, labeled "Avg: N dBm": the average of the green trace across the band, smoothed so it moves slowly\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
Yellow and magenta lines: points marked with the left and right mouse buttons, with the frequency differences to the peak and to each other\.
.IP "" 0
.P
The axis and the markers show MHz\. Pressing \fBb\fR shows BLE channel numbers instead \-\- the advertising channels 37, 38, and 39, and the data channels 0 to 36 \-\- and pressing it again goes back to MHz\. In channel mode the axis is marked on the channels themselves, so a line on the graph lines up with the number under it\. A marker names the channel nearest the point it sits on, since the sweep steps 1 MHz where the channels are 2 MHz apart\. The difference between two markers stays in MHz either way, written \fB(+4 MHz)\fR in channel mode\. The margin either side of the band has no channel, so a point out there keeps its frequency\.
.SH "KEYS AND MOUSE"
Press \fBh\fR in the window to show this list on screen\. Keys work in upper or lower case\. There are two mouse markers, one per button: clicking again moves that marker\. On a Mac trackpad, right click is a two\-finger click or Control\-click\.
.TP
\fBleft click\fR
set marker 1 (yellow) at the pointer's frequency and level
.TP
\fBright click\fR
set marker 2 (magenta); the frequency differences to marker 1 and to the peak are shown
.TP
\fBmiddle click\fR or \fBm\fR
show or hide the peak and average markers, and clear the mouse markers
.TP
\fBb\fR
label the axis and the markers with BLE channel numbers instead of MHz, or back again
.TP
\fBc\fR
clear the graph
.TP
\fBh\fR
show or hide the key help
.TP
\fBq\fR
quit
.SH "DIAGNOSTICS"
A range \fBubertooth\-specan\-ui\fR will not take is refused before the window opens, with one line starting \fBerror:\fR on standard error and exit status 1: \fBerror: invalid channel range: <value>\fR, \fBerror: channel <n> is not a valid wifi channel\fR, \fBerror: frequency of <n> MHz is below minimum frequency of <min>\fR, \fBerror: frequency of <n> MHz is above maximum frequency of <max>\fR and \fBerror: lower frequency of <n> MHz is above upper frequency of <n> MHz\fR\. They used to be \fBERROR: \|\.\|\.\|\.\fR on standard output\.
.P
A command line the argument parser will not read is refused the same way, in the lines the other tools here give for the same mistakes: \fBerror: unrecognized option '\-\-bogus'\fR for an option this tool does not have, \fBerror: unexpected argument 'extra'\fR for a word that is neither an option nor an option's value, and \fBerror: option '\-l' requires a value\fR for an option given none\. The word is quoted as the parser read it, so a run of letters it could not place is named whole: \fBerror: unrecognized option '\-Zx'\fR\. A \fB\-\-\fR ends the options, so what follows it is read as a word however it is spelled, and \fB\-\-\fR on its own asks for nothing beyond the defaults and runs\. No usage text follows any of these lines, which say what was wrong; \fB\-h\fR prints it\. These command lines used to open the window and start a sweep, the unreadable part dropped without a word\. A value the parser cannot read, as in \fB\-l abc\fR, goes the same way but keeps the parser's own words (\fBerror: argument \-l: invalid int value: 'abc'\fR); it used to print the usage text and exit 2\.
.P
A failure to start \fBubertooth\-specan\fR is reported the same way, as \fBerror: could not open an Ubertooth device\fR followed by a \fBnote:\fR naming the command, and whatever \fBubertooth\-specan\fR itself said on standard error\.
.P
A \fB\-V\fR with no standard output to print the answer to, which is what \fBubertooth\-specan\-ui \-V >&\-\fR leaves, is \fBerror: standard output has gone away\fR and exit 1\. ubertooth\-util(1) is not started there: with that descriptor closed, the first file it opened would take its number and the answer would be written into that\. A descriptor open for reading only (\fBubertooth\-specan\-ui \-V 1<&0\fR) is the same line and status, and starts no ubertooth\-util(1) either: the answer is the child's to print, and nothing but the child's own exit status would say it was refused\.
.P
The key help \fBh\fR shows goes to standard output, as what the user asked for\. The notes about an unknown or unsupported key are \fBnote:\fR lines on standard error\. The usage text \fB\-h\fR prints is the argument parser's, printed by the tool: standard output, exit 0\. The option itself is the tool's own, so that a range the tool will not take is reported even beside it, as it is in every other tool here\.
.P
A \fB\-h\fR with nowhere to print that text is \fBerror: standard output has gone away\fR and exit 1: a command that printed nothing did not do what was asked\. That covers both descriptors a shell can leave, one closed before the tool started (\fBubertooth\-specan\-ui \-h >&\-\fR) and one open for reading only (\fBubertooth\-specan\-ui \-h 1<&0\fR)\. Left to the argument parser, the first of those writes the text to standard error instead\. A \fB\-V \-h\fR that loses both halves of its answer says the line once\.
.P
With standard error gone, the \fBerror:\fR lines above are lost rather than moved: none of them falls back to standard output, where a reader or a script is entitled to find only what it asked for\. A standard error open for reading only (\fBubertooth\-specan\-ui \-l 2000 2<&0\fR), closed (\fBubertooth\-specan\-ui \-l 2000 2>&\-\fR), or a pipe whose reader has left (\fBubertooth\-specan\-ui \-l 2000 2>&1 | true\fR) all go the same way\. The exit status is then all that is left to say what happened, and it is the tool's own: it used to be 120, from the flush the interpreter makes as it exits\.
